mc(20,'Grating orders suppressed by the slit envelope','C.3',r'Monochromatic light of wavelength $\lambda$ is normally incident on a grating with separation $d=4.4\lambda$ and rectangular slit width $b=d/2$. Principal orders satisfy $d\sin\theta=n\lambda$; envelope minima satisfy $b\sin\theta=m\lambda$ for nonzero integer $m$. How many principal maxima remain after coincident envelope minima are excluded?',[r'$4$',r'$5$',r'$8$',r'$9$'],'B',r'Geometrically possible orders are $n=0,\pm1,\pm2,\pm3,\pm4$. An envelope minimum coincides when $m=nb/d=n/2$ is nonzero integer. Thus the even nonzero orders vanish, leaving $0,\pm1,\pm3$: five maxima. A omits the central maximum; C counts eight nonzero possible orders; D ignores the envelope.',11,20,svg(''.join(rect(80+i*100,60,50,130) for i in range(5))+line(80,215,180,215)+text(130,240,'d',14,'middle')+line(80,35,130,35)+text(105,25,'b = d/2',14,'middle'),600,280),'ahl')
begin('1A21');ck('A1A21 closed-pipe new fundamental',(270-180)/2,45)
mc(21,'Changing a pipe boundary','C.4',r'An open-open pipe has adjacent resonance frequencies $180\,\mathrm{Hz}$ and $270\,\mathrm{Hz}$. One end is then closed without changing its length or the sound speed. Ignoring end corrections, what is the lowest resonance frequency of the new pipe?',[r'$45\,\mathrm{Hz}$',r'$60\,\mathrm{Hz}$',r'$90\,\mathrm{Hz}$',r'$180\,\mathrm{Hz}$'],'A',r'The open-pipe spacing $90\,\mathrm{Hz}$ equals $v/(2L)$. A closed-open pipe has lowest frequency $v/(4L)=45\,\mathrm{Hz}$. B treats the two given frequencies as a closed-pipe sequence; C keeps the old fundamental; D takes the first listed resonance as fundamental.',12,21,svg(line(90,80,490,80)+line(90,170,490,170)+text(100,205,'open',14,'middle')+text(490,205,'open',14,'middle')+line(90,230,490,230)+text(290,255,'L',14,'middle'),600,280))
begin('1A22');ck('A1A22 resonance frequency lower under more damping',math.sqrt(100-10**2/2),math.sqrt(50));ck('A1A22 original resonance frequency',math.sqrt(100-4**2/2),math.sqrt(92))
mc(22,'What stronger damping changes','C.4',r'A lightly damped mechanical oscillator is driven by a sinusoidal force of fixed amplitude. Its mass and spring are unchanged, but the damping is increased while a resonance peak remains. Which change is expected in the steady-state amplitude-frequency graph?',['taller and narrower peak, shifted to higher frequency','lower and broader peak, shifted to lower frequency','lower and narrower peak, shifted to higher frequency','unchanged peak height, shifted to lower frequency'],'B',r'More damping dissipates more energy per cycle, reducing the resonant amplitude and broadening the response. The frequency of peak displacement moves below the undamped natural frequency as damping grows. A/C reverse these trends; D omits the energy-loss effect. No quantitative damping formula is required.',12,22)
begin('1A23');ck('A1A23 sound-source speed fraction',(2-1)/(2+1),1/3)
mc(23,'A source heard before and after passing','C.5',r'A stationary microphone records a source approaching directly and then receding directly at the same constant speed. The approaching frequency is twice the receding frequency. The medium is stationary. What is the source speed as a fraction of the sound speed $v$?',[r'$v/4$',r'$v/3$',r'$v/2$',r'$2v/3$'],'B',r'$f_a/f_r=(v+u)/(v-u)=2$, so $v+u=2v-2u$ and $u=v/3$. A/C/D do not satisfy the two-sided frequency ratio. Only the source moves, so this remains within the current mechanical-Doppler scope.',13,23,svg(line(60,175,550,175)+circle(200,175,17,'S')+arrow(220,135,330,135,'u')+rect(450,145,50,55,'mic')+text(300,225,'stationary medium',14,'middle'),600,270),'ahl')
begin('1A24');ck('A1A24 instantaneous gravitational power for perpendicular vectors',3*0+0*(-2),0);ck('A1A24 momentum derivative magnitude',math.hypot(0,-2),2)
mc(24,'Field direction versus instantaneous velocity','D.1',r'At one instant a probe velocity is horizontal while the gravitational field at its position is vertical downward. No other force acts. Which statement is correct at that instant?',['Kinetic energy and momentum are both constant in time.','Kinetic energy has zero instantaneous rate of change, but momentum does not.','Momentum has zero instantaneous rate of change, but kinetic energy does not.','Kinetic energy must be decreasing because gravity acts.'],'B',r'The instantaneous power $\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf v$ is zero because force and velocity are perpendicular, so $dK/dt=0$ at this instant. But $d\mathbf p/dt=\mathbf F\ne0$. A/C ignore the change of direction; D wrongly assumes every force changes speed. This is an instantaneous result, not a claim of a permanently circular orbit.',14,24,svg(circle(240,140,10)+arrow(250,140,420,140,'v')+arrow(240,152,240,240,'g',265,225)+text(240,70,'probe',14,'middle'),600,280))
begin('1A25');ck('A1A25 dissipated energy relative to initial kinetic',.5/.8-.5,.125);ck('A1A25 heat fraction of initial kinetic',(.5/.8-.5)/.5,.25)
mc(25,'Energy released in a slow orbital descent','D.1',r'A satellite slowly descends through nearly circular orbits from radius $r$ to $0.80r$. Its mass stays constant. Let its initial kinetic energy be $K$. For a circular orbit the total energy is $-GMm/(2r)$. How much energy is dissipated during the descent?',[r'$0.20K$',r'$0.25K$',r'$0.80K$',r'$1.25K$'],'B',r'Initially $E_i=-K$ and finally $E_f=-K/0.80=-1.25K$. Thus dissipation is $E_i-E_f=0.25K$, although the kinetic energy increases. A uses the fractional radius change as an energy change; C/D are unrelated retained/final-energy factors.',15,25,svg(circle(290,145,115)+circle(290,145,92)+circle(290,145,25,'M')+text(415,150,'r',14)+text(270,48,'0.80r',14,'middle'),600,290),'ahl')
begin('1A26');ck('A1A26 cancelling electric-field terms',1/(-3)**2,4/(-3-3)**2)
mc(26,'A field zero outside unequal opposite charges','D.2',r'Charges $+Q$ and $-4Q$ are fixed at $x=0$ and $x=3d$. At which coordinate is the resultant electric field zero?',[r'$x=-3d$',r'$x=-d$',r'$x=d$',r'$x=6d$'],'A',r'Between the charges their fields point in the same direction. To the left write the distance from $+Q$ as $s$; cancellation requires $Q/s^2=4Q/(s+3d)^2$, so $s+3d=2s$ and $s=3d$. Hence $x=-3d$. B gives unequal magnitudes, C lies between charges, and D is beyond the larger charge where its field dominates.',15,26,charges())
begin('1A27');ck('A1A27 external work moving a positive charge up field',2e-9*200*3,1.2e-6)
mc(27,'Work across an oblique displacement','D.2',r'A uniform electric field of strength $200\,\mathrm{V\,m^{-1}}$ is vertically downward. Point $S$ is $3.0\,\mathrm m$ above and $4.0\,\mathrm m$ right of $R$. What external work is required to move a $+2.0\,\mathrm{nC}$ charge slowly from $R$ to $S$?',[r'$-2.0\,\mathrm{\mu J}$',r'$-1.2\,\mathrm{\mu J}$',r'$+1.2\,\mathrm{\mu J}$',r'$+2.0\,\mathrm{\mu J}$'],'C',r'$V_S-V_R=+E(3.0)=600\,\mathrm V$, because potential increases against the field. The external work equals $q\Delta V=1.2\,\mathrm{\mu J}$. A/D use the full five-metre path; A/B also reverse the sign. Horizontal displacement adds no work.',16,27,svg(circle(160,210,5)+text(140,235,'R')+circle(400,50,5)+text(415,55,'S')+line(160,210,400,210,True)+line(400,210,400,50,True)+text(270,235,'4.0 m')+text(420,145,'3.0 m')+arrow(80,50,80,170,'E',50,90),600,280),'ahl')
begin('1A28');ck('A1A28 specific charge from curvature slope',1/(.4*(.6-.2)/(3e5-1e5)),1.25e6)
mc(28,'Reading a curvature calibration','D.3',r'The graph shows the orbit radius of a nonrelativistic ion in a uniform field $B=0.40\,\mathrm T$, perpendicular to its velocity. What is the ion\'s charge-to-mass ratio magnitude?',[r'$1.25\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{C\,kg^{-1}}$',r'$1.25\times10^5\,\mathrm{C\,kg^{-1}}$',r'$1.25\times10^6\,\mathrm{C\,kg^{-1}}$',r'$5.00\times10^6\,\mathrm{C\,kg^{-1}}$'],'C',r'$r=mv/(|q|B)$, so the SI gradient is $\Delta r/\Delta v=0.40/(2.0\times10^5)=2.0\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm s$. Thus $|q|/m=1/(B\,\mathrm{gradient})=1.25\times10^6\,\mathrm{C\,kg^{-1}}$. A takes the reciprocal quantity; B/D mis-handle the graph scale or field.',16,28,plot([(0,0),(3,.6)],(0,3.5),(0,.7),'speed / (10⁵ m s⁻¹)','orbit radius / m',[0,1,2,3],[0,.2,.4,.6]))
begin('1A29');ck('A1A29 selected-ion radius ratio',4/2,2)
mc(29,'A velocity selector followed by a magnet','D.3',r'Protons and alpha particles both pass undeflected through the same perpendicular electric and magnetic fields. They then enter a second, purely magnetic region at right angles to its field. An alpha particle has four times a proton\'s mass and twice its charge. What is $r_\alpha/r_p$ in the second region?',[r'$1/2$',r'$1$',r'$2$',r'$4$'],'C',r'Undeflected motion selects $v=E/B$, independent of mass and charge. In the second region $r=mv/(qB)$, so $r_\alpha/r_p=4/2=2$. A reverses the ratio, B keeps only the common speed, and D neglects the alpha charge.',17,29,svg(rect(90,55,200,170,'selector')+rect(365,55,160,170,'B only')+arrow(40,140,80,140,'v')+arrow(295,140,355,140)+text(185,245,'E perpendicular to B',14,'middle'),600,280))
begin('1A30');ck('A1A30 clockwise top-segment radial force y',-1,-1)
mc(30,'An expanding ring in a steady field','D.4',r'A flexible conducting ring lies in a steady uniform magnetic field directed out of the page. An external agent starts increasing its area. Which induced conventional current and magnetic-force tendency oppose the change?',['clockwise current; tendency to contract','clockwise current; tendency to expand','counterclockwise current; tendency to contract','counterclockwise current; tendency to expand'],'A',r'Outward flux increases as area increases. Lenz\'s law requires an inward induced field, hence clockwise current. Each current segment experiences an inward radial magnetic force, opposing expansion; the vector sum of these forces is zero. B reverses the stress; C/D reverse the induced field.',17,30,svg(fielddots(130,490,60,210)+'<circle cx="310" cy="145" r="75" fill="none" stroke="#202020" stroke-width="2"/>'+arrow(400,145,450,145,'expansion',460,125)+text(310,270,'B out of page',14,'middle'),600,290),'ahl')
begin('1A31');ck('A1A31 signed mean emf',-50*(-.004)/6,1/30);ck('A1A31 time mean magnitude',50*(.006+.010)/6,2/15)
mc(31,'Signed and absolute induced emfs','D.4',r'A $50$-turn coil has the flux per turn shown. Positive emf is defined by $\mathcal E=-N\Delta\Phi/\Delta t$. Over the full six seconds, what are the signed mean emf and the mean magnitude of emf?',[r'$+0.0333\,\mathrm V,\ 0.133\,\mathrm V$',r'$+0.133\,\mathrm V,\ 0.0333\,\mathrm V$',r'$-0.0333\,\mathrm V,\ 0.133\,\mathrm V$',r'$+0.200\,\mathrm V,\ 0.200\,\mathrm V$'],'A',r'The signed integral is $-N(\Phi_f-\Phi_i)=+0.20\,\mathrm{V\,s}$, giving $+0.0333\,\mathrm V$. For the magnitude, total absolute flux change is $(6+10)\,\mathrm{mWb}$, so the mean is $50(0.016)/6=0.133\,\mathrm V$. B swaps the two averages, C misses Faraday\'s sign, and D does not time-average the total change.',18,31,plot([(0,0),(2,6),(5,6),(6,-4)],(0,6),(-5,7),'time / s','flux per turn / mWb',[0,2,5,6],[-4,0,6]),'ahl')
begin('1A32');ck('A1A32 thin-foil probability ratio',(-math.expm1(-.0005))/(-math.expm1(-.001)),.5001249999974,1e-10)
mc(32,'Thinner foil in a scattering experiment','E.1',r'An alpha-scattering experiment is repeated with the same beam energy and metal, but a foil of half the thickness. Both foils are sufficiently thin that single nuclear scattering dominates. Which change is expected?',['approximately half as many large-angle events; most particles still pass nearly straight through','approximately twice as many large-angle events; most particles still pass nearly straight through','unchanged large-angle fraction; all transmitted particles have half their original speed','approximately half as many large-angle events; every particle loses half its kinetic energy'],'A',r'At low scattering probability the number of target nuclei met is proportional to thickness, so the large-angle fraction approximately halves. Most atoms are largely empty space and most particles still pass nearly straight through. B reverses the target-number effect; C/D invent a uniform large energy loss.',18,32)
begin('1A33');ck('A1A33 ionization-to-line-energy ratio',(13.6/4)/(13.6*(1/4-1/9)),9/5)
mc(33,'Ionization compared with one hydrogen line','E.1',r'Hydrogen has $E_n=-13.6/n^2\,\mathrm{eV}$. What is the ratio of the energy required to ionize an atom initially in $n=2$ to the energy of a photon emitted in $n=3\to2$?',[r'$5/9$',r'$9/5$',r'$4$',r'$36/5$'],'B',r'Ionization from $n=2$ needs $13.6/4$eV. The line energy is $13.6(1/4-1/9)=13.6(5/36)$eV. Their ratio is $(1/4)/(5/36)=9/5$. A reverses the ratio; C/D use an incorrect initial level or subtraction.',19,33,svg(line(160,45,460,45)+text(470,50,'0 eV')+line(160,105,460,105)+text(470,110,'n = 3')+line(160,165,460,165)+text(470,170,'n = 2')+line(160,240,460,240)+text(470,245,'n = 1')+text(280,278,'Levels schematic; not to scale',13,'middle'),600,300),'ahl')
begin('1A34');ck('A1A34 photon count rate ratio for half wavelength',.5,.5);ck('A1A34 extra kinetic energy beyond twice original for sample work function',(2*3-2)-2*(3-2),2)
mc(34,'Fixed optical power at a shorter wavelength','E.2',r'A metal is illuminated above threshold by monochromatic light. Its wavelength is halved while total incident power and illuminated area remain fixed. Assume every incident photon releases one collected electron. Which change occurs?',['saturation current doubles; maximum kinetic energy doubles','saturation current halves; maximum kinetic energy is more than doubled','saturation current halves; maximum kinetic energy is less than doubled','saturation current is unchanged; maximum kinetic energy doubles'],'B',r'Photon energy doubles, so fixed optical power supplies half as many photons each second and the saturation current halves. If the original photon energy is $E$ and work function $\Phi>0$, $K_2=2E-\Phi=2(E-\Phi)+\Phi>2K_1$. A/D confuse power with photon rate; C reverses the work-function offset.',19,34,level='ahl')
begin('1A35');ck('A1A35 Compton recoil-energy ratio',(1-1/3)/(1-1/2),4/3)
mc(35,'Two Compton scattering angles','E.2',r'A photon has initial wavelength equal to the electron Compton wavelength $\lambda_C=h/(m_ec)$. It scatters from an initially stationary free electron. What is the ratio of electron kinetic energy for photon scattering angle $180^\circ$ to that for $90^\circ$?',[r'$2/3$',r'$1$',r'$4/3$',r'$2$'],'C',r'Compton shifts give final wavelengths $3\lambda_C$ at $180^\circ$ and $2\lambda_C$ at $90^\circ$. Relative to initial photon energy $E_i$, the transferred energies are $2E_i/3$ and $E_i/2$. Their ratio is $4/3$. A reverses the comparison, B ignores angle, and D wrongly makes transferred energy proportional to the wavelength shift.',20,35,svg(arrow(60,140,260,140,'incident photon')+circle(280,140,10,'e')+arrow(295,130,430,45,'scattered photon',440,30)+text(360,115,'θ')+line(295,140,490,140,True),600,270),'ahl')
begin('1A36');ck('A1A36 de Broglie wavelength ratio',1/math.sqrt(4*2),1/(2*math.sqrt(2)))
mc(36,'Mass and charge in electron-wave analogues','E.2',r'Two nonrelativistic particles start from rest and accelerate through the same potential difference. Particle $X$ has mass $m$ and charge $q$; particle $Y$ has mass $4m$ and charge $2q$, of the same sign. What is $\lambda_Y/\lambda_X$?',[r'$1/4$',r'$1/2$',r'$1/(2\sqrt2)$',r'$\sqrt2$'],'C',r'$K=qV$ and $p=\sqrt{2mK}$, so $p_Y/p_X=\sqrt{4\times2}=2\sqrt2$. With $\lambda=h/p$, the wavelength ratio is $1/(2\sqrt2)$. A/B omit the charge or misuse the square root; D reverses or incompletely applies the mass/charge dependence.',20,36,level='ahl')
begin('1A37');bg=2*70-110;ck('A1A37 background count rate',bg,30);ck('A1A37 raw count rate after three half lives',bg+(110-bg)/8,40)
mc(37,'A background hidden in decay readings','E.3',r'A detector reads $110$, $70$ and $50\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at times $0$, $T$ and $2T$, where $T$ is the source half-life. Background is constant and detector response is linear. What raw count rate is expected at $3T$?',[r'$30\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$',r'$35\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$',r'$40\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$',r'$45\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$'],'C',r'Writing raw rate as $B+S/2^n$, $B=2(70)-110=30\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and $S=80\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. At $3T$ the source contributes $10$ and raw rate is $40$. A is background alone, B halves a background-contaminated difference incorrectly, and D assumes a linear decrease.',20,37)
begin('1A38');ck('A1A38 neutrons in balanced fission reaction',240-144-93,3);ck('A1A38 required beta-minus decays',40-38,2)
mc(38,'Following a fission fragment','E.4',r'One possible fission is $^{239}_{94}\mathrm{Pu}+{}^1_0n\to{}^{144}_{56}\mathrm{Ba}+{}^{93}_{38}\mathrm{Sr}+3{}^1_0n$. The $^{93}\mathrm{Sr}$ fragment later becomes $^{93}_{40}\mathrm{Zr}$ through beta decays without other nuclear changes. What emissions are required?',['one electron and one antineutrino','two electrons and two antineutrinos','two positrons and two neutrinos','three electrons and three antineutrinos'],'B',r'At unchanged mass number, each beta-minus decay raises proton number by one and emits an electron plus antineutrino. Raising $Z$ from $38$ to $40$ therefore requires two such decays. A/C/D have the wrong number or sign of charge change; the preceding fission conserves both $A$ and $Z$.',21,38)
begin('1A39');ck('A1A39 giant-to-hot-star radius ratio',math.sqrt(100)*(10000/5000)**2,40)
mc(39,'Radii on an HR diagram','E.5',r'The HR diagram places star $X$ at $(10000\,\mathrm K,1L_\odot)$ and star $Y$ at $(5000\,\mathrm K,100L_\odot)$. Treat both as spherical black bodies. What is $R_Y/R_X$?',[r'$0.40$',r'$2.5$',r'$10$',r'$40$'],'D',r'From $L=4\pi R^2\sigma T^4$, $R_Y/R_X=\sqrt{100}(10000/5000)^2=40$. A reverses luminosity, B mishandles the temperature power, and C ignores temperature. A cooler yet more luminous star must have much larger area.',21,39,plot([],(0,1),(0,3),'temperature decreases to right','log₁₀(L / L☉)',[],[0,1,2,3],labels=[((.2,0),'X: 10000 K',-12,-15),((.7,2),'Y: 5000 K',-35,-15)]))
begin('1A40');distance=1/(.08/2);lum=4*math.pi*(distance*3.086e16)**2*6e-10;ck('A1A40 parallax distance',distance,25);ck('A1A40 stellar luminosity',lum,4.487794636624e27,2e15)
mc(40,'From a measured angular sweep to luminosity','E.5',r'The maximum change in a star\'s apparent direction between observations six months apart is $0.080\,\mathrm{arcsec}$. Its apparent brightness is $6.0\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$. Using $1\,\mathrm{pc}=3.086\times10^{16}\,\mathrm m$, what is the star\'s luminosity?',[r'$1.12\times10^{27}\,\mathrm W$',r'$4.49\times10^{27}\,\mathrm W$',r'$1.79\times10^{28}\,\mathrm W$',r'$3.77\times10^{10}\,\mathrm W$'],'B',r'The measured sweep is $2p$, so $p=0.040$arcsec and $d=25$pc. Then $L=4\pi d^2b=4.49\times10^{27}\,\mathrm W$. A uses the full sweep as parallax, C halves parallax a second time, and D fails to convert parsecs to metres.',22,40)

