wq('1B',1,'Settling-bead viscosity and independent density','A.2',r'''A model laboratory dataset is used to investigate a viscous liquid. A solid calibration body of known volume $4.00\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{m^3}$ has balance readings $0.03120\,\mathrm{kg}$ in air and $0.02640\,\mathrm{kg}$ when fully immersed. Ignore air buoyancy. Use $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$.

Spherical steel beads of density $\rho_s=7800\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ settle through the same liquid. The plotted terminal speeds have uncertainty $\pm0.5\,\mathrm{mm\,s^{-1}}$; radius-squared uncertainties are negligible. After assessing the graph, use the reported gradient $s=(12.0\pm0.4)\,(\mathrm{mm\,s^{-1}})/\mathrm{mm^2}$ for subsequent calculations. The independent density measurement gives $\rho_l=(1200\pm75)\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$.

For terminal Stokes motion, $v_t=2g(\rho_s-\rho_l)r^2/(9\eta)$. Treat $g$ and $\rho_s$ as exact. A second test examines the validity of the Stokes model using the supplied diagnostic $\mathrm{Re}=2\rho_lrv_t/\eta$; the model is more reliable when $\mathrm{Re}\ll1$.''',[
('a',r'Calculate the liquid density from the two balance readings and the calibration-body volume.',2),
('b',r'Explain why the graph is approximately consistent with $v_t\propto r^2$, and convert the reported gradient to SI units.',2),
('c',r'Determine $\eta$ and its absolute uncertainty by adding the relevant fractional uncertainties. Give suitable units and precision.',3),
('d',r'Use the largest bead to evaluate the Stokes assumption. Suggest one change that reduces this limitation, and explain why terminal speed must be checked separately.',3)
],[('a',[('M1',r'The lost apparent mass is $0.00480\,\mathrm{kg}$ and buoyancy gives $(m_{air}-m_{immersed})g=\rho_lVg$.'),('A1',r'$\rho_l=0.00480/(4.00\times10^{-6})=1200\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$.')]),
('b',[('R1',r'The plotted error bars are consistent with a straight line through the origin; this supports the proportional model over this measured range, without proving it universally.'),('A1',r'$1\,(\mathrm{mm\,s^{-1}})/\mathrm{mm^2}=10^3\,\mathrm{m^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$, so $s=1.20\times10^4\,\mathrm{m^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$.')]),
('c',[('M1',r'$\eta=2g(\rho_s-\rho_l)/(9s)=1.199\,\mathrm{Pa\,s}$.'),('M1',r'$\Delta\eta/\eta=75/(7800-1200)+0.4/12.0=0.044697$. The numerator is a density difference, so use its absolute uncertainty before dividing.'),('A1',r'$\eta=(1.20\pm0.05)\,\mathrm{Pa\,s}$; an appropriately rounded equivalent such as $1.199\pm0.054$ is accepted.')]),
('d',[('M1',r'The largest bead has $r=\sqrt{3.24}\,\mathrm{mm}=1.8\,\mathrm{mm}$ and $v_t=0.0388\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, giving $\mathrm{Re}=0.140$.'),('R1',r'This is below one but not arbitrarily small; smaller beads reduce $r$ and $v_t$, lowering Re and improving the creeping-flow approximation.'),('R1',r'Low Re alone does not prove terminal motion. Check that equal later time intervals give equal displacements, or that a late displacement-time trace has constant gradient; otherwise the force balance used for $v_t$ is invalid.')])],
[src(1,'1a-c','Buoyancy balance and independent density','s.pdf'),src(2,'1d-e','Terminal-motion graph interpretation','s.pdf'),src(4,'1f-g','Viscosity uncertainty and model evaluation','s.pdf')],fig)

