wq('1B',2,'Radiative power with a chamber background','B.1',r'''A model vacuum-chamber investigation tests radiative power from a coated tile. Its area is known and its emissivity is assumed temperature-independent. Chamber walls stay at $T_0=300\,\mathrm K$. The measured net outward radiative power per area is $J=\varepsilon\sigma(T^4-T_0^4)$, where $\sigma=5.67\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}\,K^{-4}}$. Conduction through supports is negligible.

| $T/\mathrm K$ | 600 | 700 | 800 | 900 | 1000 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| $J/(\mathrm{kW\,m^{-2}})$ | 5.50 | 10.5 | 18.2 | 29.4 | 45.0 |

Each measured $J$ has uncertainty $\pm0.50\,\mathrm{kW\,m^{-2}}$. The graph uses $X=(T^4-T_0^4)/(10^{11}\,\mathrm{K^4})$. A fit through the origin gives gradient $(4.54\pm0.08)\,\mathrm{kW\,m^{-2}}$ per unit $X$. Treat temperature uncertainties as negligible in parts(a)–(c).''',[
('a',r'Calculate $X$ for the $800\,\mathrm K$ measurement. Explain why a straight-line plot against $X$ is appropriate.',2),
('b',r'Use the fitted gradient to determine the emissivity and its absolute uncertainty.',3),
('c',r'If the same data were plotted as $J$ against $T^4$, determine the expected vertical intercept in $\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$ and explain its sign.',2),
('d',r'For a tile at $800\,\mathrm K$, the chamber is cooled to $150\,\mathrm K$. Predict the fractional change in $J$. Explain why fitting $\log J$ against $\log T$ without a background correction need not yield exactly4.',3)
],[('a',[('A1',r'$X=(800^4-300^4)/10^{11}=4.015$.'),('R1',r'The stated model has $J=(\varepsilon\sigma\,10^{11})X$, giving a straight line through the origin if the model and constant emissivity apply.')]),
('b',[('M1',r'Convert the gradient to $4.54\times10^3\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$ and use $\varepsilon=s/(\sigma10^{11})$.'),('A1',r'$\varepsilon=0.800705$.'),('A1',r'$\Delta\varepsilon=\varepsilon(0.08/4.54)=0.0141$, so $\varepsilon=0.801\pm0.014$ or an appropriate equivalent.')]),
('c',[('A1',r'The extrapolated intercept is $-\varepsilon\sigma T_0^4=-367.74\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$.'),('R1',r'It represents absorption of chamber radiation subtracted from the tile emission; the negative extrapolation at $T=0$ is not negative gross emitted power.')]),
('d',[('M1',r'$J_{new}/J_{old}=(800^4-150^4)/(800^4-300^4)=1.01891$.'),('A1',r'The net outward power increases by about $1.89\%$.'),('R1',r'$J$ is proportional to $T^4-T_0^4$, rather than $T^4$. Thus $\log J=\log(\varepsilon\sigma)+\log(T^4-T_0^4)$ and the background term prevents an exact slope4 unless it is negligible or corrected.')])],
[src(5,'2a','Transforming thermal-radiation measurements','s.pdf'),src(6,'2b','Choosing a fitted representation','s.pdf'),src(7,'2c-e','Interpreting fit constants and improving inference','s.pdf')],fig,refs=['B.1','B.2'])

