wq('2',5,'Recoil energy and a nuclear-scale scattering test','E.1',r'''An initially stationary $^{212}_{84}\mathrm{Po}$ nucleus emits an alpha particle and becomes a lead nucleus. The decay releases $8.95\,\mathrm{MeV}$ of kinetic energy. For recoil calculations take the daughter-to-alpha mass ratio as $208:4$ and use nonrelativistic mechanics.

An alpha particle from this decay is subsequently directed head-on towards a stationary $^{118}_{50}\mathrm{Sn}$ nucleus. For this scattering calculation neglect the tin recoil and use electrostatic repulsion until nuclear-scale distances are reached. Use $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2\,C^{-2}}$, $e=1.60\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C$ and $R=1.20\times10^{-15}A^{1/3}\,\mathrm m$.''',[
('a',r'Write the nuclear decay equation and state the momentum relationship between the products.',2),
('b',r'Determine the kinetic energies of the alpha particle and lead nucleus.',3),
('c',r'Calculate the alpha particle distance of closest approach to the tin nucleus.',3),
('d',r'Estimate the incident alpha kinetic energy needed for the electrostatic closest approach to equal the tin nuclear radius. Explain why the simple model should be treated cautiously at that scale.',2)
],[('a',[('A1',r'$^{212}_{84}\mathrm{Po}\to{}^{208}_{82}\mathrm{Pb}+{}^4_2\mathrm{He}$.'),('R1',r'The momenta are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction because the parent initially has zero momentum.')]),
('b',[('M1',r'With equal $p$, $K=p^2/(2m)$ gives $K_\alpha/K_{Pb}=208/4=52$.'),('M1',r'The energies sum to $8.95\,\mathrm{MeV}$, so $K_\alpha=8.95(208/212)$ and $K_{Pb}=8.95(4/212)$.'),('A1',r'$K_\alpha=8.78113\,\mathrm{MeV}\approx8.78\,\mathrm{MeV}$ and $K_{Pb}=0.168868\,\mathrm{MeV}\approx0.169\,\mathrm{MeV}$.')]),
('c',[('M1',r'At closest approach $K_\alpha=k(2e)(50e)/r_{min}$.'),('M1',r'Convert $8.78113\,\mathrm{MeV}$ to joules using $10^6e$.'),('A1',r'$r_{min}=1.63806\times10^{-14}\,\mathrm m\approx1.64\times10^{-14}\,\mathrm m$.')]),
('d',[('M1',r'$R_{Sn}=1.20\times10^{-15}(118)^{1/3}=5.88584\times10^{-15}\,\mathrm m$ and $K=k(100e^2)/R_{Sn}\approx24.4\,\mathrm{MeV}$.'),('R1',r'This extrapolation reaches the finite-sized nucleus, where the point-charge/electrostatic-only approximation can fail and nuclear interactions become relevant; it is a scale estimate, not a guaranteed scattering prediction.')])],
[src(12,'5a','Momentum sharing in alpha recoil','1.pdf'),src(13,'5b','Rutherford head-on energy balance','1.pdf'),src(14,'5b(iv)','Nuclear radius scaling','1.pdf')],fig,'ahl',['E.1','E.3','A.2'])

begin('2Q6');hexp=1.240e-6*e/c;lamth=1e-6*1.24/2.6;Vs=1.24*(1e-6/300e-9)-2.6;db=h/math.sqrt(2*me*e*Vs)
